# Undo the repo-root's unanchored build-output patterns inside Unity territory.
# The root .gitignore's bare `bin/ obj/ build/ dist/` match at ANY depth, so an
# imported art pack shipping a folder of that name would vanish from git with no
# warning -- not even listed as ignored. The anchored Unity rules below cover
# what genuinely needs ignoring here. See issue #15.
!bin/
!obj/
!build/
!dist/

# This .gitignore file should be placed at the root of your Unity project directory
#
# Get latest from https://github.com/github/gitignore/blob/main/Unity.gitignore
#
# Recommended: add any editor/OS/tool-specific ignore rules from the Global/ templates as needed.
# See: https://github.com/github/gitignore/tree/main/Global
#
.utmp/
/[Ll]ibrary/
/[Tt]emp/
/[Oo]bj/
/[Bb]uild/
/[Bb]uilds/
/[Ll]ogs/
/[Uu]ser[Ss]ettings/
*.log

# By default unity supports Blender asset imports, *.blend1 blender files do not need to be commited to version control.
*.blend1
*.blend1.meta

# MemoryCaptures can get excessive in size.
# They also could contain extremely sensitive data
/[Mm]emoryCaptures/

# Recordings can get excessive in size
/[Rr]ecordings/

# Uncomment this line if you wish to ignore the asset store tools plugin
# /[Aa]ssets/AssetStoreTools*

# Autogenerated Jetbrains Rider plugin
/[Aa]ssets/Plugins/Editor/JetBrains*
# Jetbrains Rider personal-layer settings
*.DotSettings.user

# Visual Studio cache directory
.vs/

# Gradle cache directory
.gradle/

# Autogenerated VS/MD/Consulo solution and project files
ExportedObj/
.consulo/
*.csproj
*.unityproj
*.sln
*.slnx
*.suo
*.tmp
*.user
*.userprefs
*.pidb
*.booproj
*.svd
*.pdb
*.mdb
*.opendb
*.VC.db

# Unity3D generated meta files
*.pidb.meta
*.pdb.meta
*.mdb.meta

# Unity3D generated file on crash reports
sysinfo.txt

# Mono auto generated files
mono_crash.*

# Builds
*.apk
*.aab
*.unitypackage
*.unitypackage.meta
*.app

# Crashlytics generated file
crashlytics-build.properties

# TestRunner generated files
InitTestScene*.unity*

# Addressables default ignores, before user customizations
/ServerData
/[Aa]ssets/StreamingAssets/aa*
/[Aa]ssets/AddressableAssetsData/link.xml*
/[Aa]ssets/Addressables_Temp*
# By default, Addressables content builds will generate addressables_content_state.bin
# files in platform-specific subfolders, for example:
# /Assets/AddressableAssetsData/OSX/addressables_content_state.bin
/[Aa]ssets/AddressableAssetsData/*/*.bin*

# Visual Scripting auto-generated files
/[Aa]ssets/Unity.VisualScripting.Generated/VisualScripting.Flow/UnitOptions.db
/[Aa]ssets/Unity.VisualScripting.Generated/VisualScripting.Flow/UnitOptions.db.meta
/[Aa]ssets/Unity.VisualScripting.Generated/VisualScripting.Core/Property Providers
/[Aa]ssets/Unity.VisualScripting.Generated/VisualScripting.Core/Property Providers.meta

# Auto-generated scenes by play mode tests
/[Aa]ssets/[Ii]nit[Tt]est[Ss]cene*.unity*

# Auto-generated cache in Assets folder
/[Aa]ssets/[Ss]ceneDependencyCache*

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Project-specific negations -- see issue #15
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The sim plug-in is a committed build artefact, not a build leftover.
# Unity deletes orphaned .meta files, so an ignored DLL cannot keep its
# committed .meta -- and Sim.dll.meta is where "Auto Reference off" lives.
# Sim.pdb is needed on disk beside the DLL for the debugger to step from view
# code into sim code. Its .meta is negated too so that "commit every .meta"
# stays an exceptionless rule.
!/Packages/com.ssalter.sim/Runtime/Sim.pdb
!/Packages/com.ssalter.sim/Runtime/Sim.pdb.meta

# The throwaway hot-reload probe (tools/hotreload-probe/) is generated into the
# project and must never be committed -- it is a stand-in for Sim.dll, and a
# committed stand-in is exactly the thing someone later mistakes for the real
# plug-in. Ignored by construction rather than by remembering.
#
# NOTE: this ignores the probe's FILES, and that is all a .gitignore can do.
# It cannot stop packages-lock.json -- a TRACKED file -- from recording the
# probe as a dependency while it is installed. It did exactly that once (see
# issue #29), and the lockfile then named a package no clone could ever
# contain. Expect a lockfile diff for as long as the probe is installed, and
# never commit it. Same shape as the Sim.dll.meta hazard above: ignore rules
# govern untracked files, not what a tracked file says about them.
#
# Two rules are enough because the probe now generates nothing outside its own
# package. It used to also write an Editor script into Assets/Editor/, and
# Unity then generated an Assets/Editor.meta that no rule here matched -- so
# the probe did leave one file for `git add -A` to commit. Naming that stray
# .meta would have been the wrong fix: Assets/Editor/ is an ordinary Unity
# folder the real project will want tracked, and an ignored .meta beside a
# tracked folder is the hazard the Sim.pdb.meta negation above exists to
# avoid. The probe's logger moved inside the package instead. See issue #34.
/Packages/com.ssalter.hotreloadprobe/
/Packages/com.ssalter.hotreloadprobe.meta

# Scene-template state: Unity writes this into ProjectSettings/, but its
# contents are UserSettings/-class -- per-user template pin states plus a
# type list re-derived from whatever packages are installed. Nothing
# references it, it has no .meta, and Unity rebuilds it on demand, so none of
# the reasons that force Sim.dll to be committed apply. Ignored so it stops
# showing up untracked in every clone forever, which is how people learn to
# stop reading `git status`.
/ProjectSettings/SceneTemplateSettings.json
